she would have been in her late teens by  now but instead Molly Russell's life  ended when she was a child  today her family listened as the inquest  into her death examined whether the  policies of Instagram's parent company  meta contributed to her death  Elizabeth Lagoon who heads up health and  well-being policy at the company said  she was confident current policies  protect young people  she said I'm not aware of specific  research on the impact of content that  would be very difficult research to  undertake with ethical considerations  we are confident our policies do  consider the needs of our youngest users  some of the content Molly had liked or  saved on her Instagram account were  shown to the court they glamorize harm  to young people and were allegedly so  distressing to view that the lawyers  involved had discussed editing them  first the coroner gave those present at  the inquest the option to leave if they  were likely to be affected by the  material but he said Molly had no such  choice and that we were in effect  editing material for adult viewing which  was widely available in an unedited form  for a child  meta is due to return next week to  answer more questions as the inquest  into Molly Russell's death continues  Sadia Chaudhary Sky News in North London
 

